#!/usr/bin/env python3
from pathlib import Path
import json,pandas as pd
from ct_peak_atlas import OUT

def main():
 p=json.loads((OUT/'provenance.json').read_text());d=pd.read_csv(OUT/'model_diagnostics.csv');s=pd.read_csv(OUT/'embedding_stability.csv');v=json.loads((OUT/'validation.json').read_text()) if (OUT/'validation.json').exists() else None
 full=(OUT/'full_ct_peak_atlas.h5').exists()
 tables=d[['name','n_features','n_pcs','pca_variance_fraction','neighborhood_recall_mean','trustworthiness','pairwise_distance_spearman']].to_html(index=False,float_format=lambda x:f'{x:.3f}')
 stability=s.to_html(index=False,float_format=lambda x:f'{x:.3f}')
 body=f'''<!doctype html><html><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Celltype × stage fixed peak atlas</title><style>body{{font-family:system-ui,sans-serif;max-width:1250px;margin:32px auto;padding:0 20px;color:#243244;line-height:1.5}}h1,h2{{line-height:1.2}}img{{max-width:100%;border:1px solid #ddd}}table{{border-collapse:collapse;font-size:13px;width:100%}}td,th{{padding:7px;border:1px solid #ddd;text-align:right}}p.note{{color:#526170}}pre{{overflow:auto;background:#f3f6f8;padding:15px}}.finding{{padding:18px;background:#eef5f9;border-left:5px solid #246a8a}}</style></head><body><h1>Rebuilding the fixed peak atlas from celltype × stage pseudobulks</h1>
<div class="finding"><strong>The proposed mismatch is real:</strong> the celltype and Leiden source objects contain different count matrices but exactly identical saved PCA, 2D UMAP, and 3D UMAP coordinates. We rebuilt matched maps from their actual accessibility matrices. Celltype aggregation aligns the geometry with the temporal analysis; it does not by itself establish a chromatin velocity field.</div>
<p><a href="ct_peak_atlas_explorer.html">Open offline interactive explorer</a> · <a href="model_diagnostics.csv">Model diagnostics</a> · <a href="embedding_stability.csv">Stability comparisons</a> · <a href="provenance.json">Source provenance</a> · <a href="validation.json">Validation</a></p>
<h2>What changed</h2><p>One observation is one genomic peak represented by its complete developmental course. This is distinct from the earlier shared peak × stage state atlas, where each peak had several positions. All eight new fits use the same 20,000 training peaks, 5,000 disjoint held-out peaks, and 56 marker extras. Their 190 existing pseudobulk columns remain in source order; no missing combination is filled with zero. Main features are log1p(CPM), using raw counts divided by the original full-peak library depth ×1,000,000. PCA uses global feature centering without z-scoring or whitening. The default retains 50 PCs; all 190 PCs are a sensitivity analysis.</p>
<p>The existing source normalization used a common library size of approximately 7.03M in the celltype object and 7.13M in the Leiden object, so we reconstructed both at a common CPM scale. All 4,760,640 sampled CT CPM values exactly match the earlier temporal-analysis observations after reindexing.</p>
<h2>Controlled source comparison</h2><p>Both matrices have 190 columns, but they are not relabelled copies: only 25 columns are exactly equal after best correlation matching on all 25,056 sampled peaks. The median matched raw-count correlation is{p['source_matrix_comparison']['median_matched_raw_correlation']:.4f}; substantial overlap is therefore expected. At every stage, both partitions have identical total cell counts and sequencing coverage. Their sampled per-peak stage-total raw counts are identical. This comparison changes how the same stage-specific cell pools are grouped.</p>
<h2>Matched maps</h2><img src="01_fixed_peak_atlas_comparison.png"><p class="note">Colors are shared across rows and computed from all 190 celltype × stage features. Each fit has arbitrary position and orientation. The original saved map was trained on the complete peak set; new comparisons use the common 20,000-peak training set. Main maps use k=30, min_dist=0.3; the broader setting uses k=60, min_dist=0.6.</p>
<h2>What the plots suggest</h2><ul><li>Celltype log1p(CPM) and matched Leiden log1p(CPM) both show a broad curved continuum with finer fingers. Aggregation changes the geometry, but it does not automatically collapse the fingers into a few major branches.</li><li>Removing 14 pseudobulk columns with fewer than 20 cells removes several fine fingers. This is a useful sensitivity result, not proof that those fingers are noise: it also removes genuine rare-celltype features, including all six primordial-germ-cell columns. The one-cell optic-cup 10 hpf column is excluded only in this sensitivity.</li><li>Linear CPM and log1p(CPM) emphasize different aspects of accessibility. Linear CPM preserves more immediate neighbors in its own feature space but has poor global distance-rank preservation. These fidelity scores cannot identify which representation is biologically correct.</li><li>The broadly accessible, low-specificity end is a descriptive feature of the geometry. Neither its position nor any apparent branches impose an opening/closing direction.</li></ul>
<h2>2D and 3D fidelity</h2>{tables}<img src="03_embedding_diagnostics.png"><p class="note">Diagnostics use the same 1,500 held-out peaks with neighbors recomputed inside this subset. Each model is evaluated against its own full feature representation. CT log 2D retains 94.0% of state variance in its first 50 PCs; its held-out 15-neighbor recall is 0.191 and distance-rank correlation 0.529. The 3D values improve to 0.227 and 0.575. These are moderate distortions, so fine geometry warrants caution.</p>
<h2>Stability relative to main CT log 2D</h2>{stability}<p>The second seed preserves broad distance relationships well (ρ≈0.915), while sharing about 63% of 15-neighbor sets with the baseline. Broader settings giveρ≈0.908 and 48% neighbor overlap. Reproducibility of a layout does not validate a temporal direction.</p>
<h2>3D fixed peak atlas</h2><img src="02_ct_peak_atlas_3d.png">
<h2>Full-peak expansion</h2><p>{'The frozen 20,000-landmark-trained PCA/UMAP models have now mapped all 640,830 peaks in 2D and 3D. Every sampled coordinate is preserved bitwise; all coordinates are finite. The full arrays are in full_ct_peak_atlas.h5. This is a landmark projection, not a UMAP refit on all peaks.' if full else 'Full 640,830-peak projection through the frozen models is running separately. It will preserve every existing sampled coordinate exactly. This report currently describes the completed 25,056-peak comparison.'}</p>
<h2>Reproducibility and validation</h2><p>{'Passed: '+str(len(v['models']))+' frozen models; exact source normalization and previous-input matching; '+str(v['viewer_numeric_arrays'])+' viewer numeric arrays containing '+format(v['viewer_numeric_values'],',')+' exact values. Offline browser behavior checks include every map, 2D/3D, condition colors, peak/gene search, and missing/low-cell conditions.' if v else 'Validation summary will be added after final browser and data checks.'}</p><pre>python ct_peak_atlas.py prepare
python ct_peak_atlas.py run --model ct_log_2d
# Repeat --model for the eight declared settings in SPECS.
python ct_peak_atlas.py summary
python ct_peak_atlas_viewer.py
node check_ct_peak_atlas_viewer.cjs
python validate_ct_peak_atlas.py
python ct_peak_atlas_full.py prepare
python ct_peak_atlas_full.py run --dim 2
python ct_peak_atlas_full.py run --dim 3
python ct_peak_atlas_full.py finish</pre>
<p class="note">Input source files remain unchanged. Aligned sampled feature arrays are in aligned_features.h5; model coordinates and reusable PCA/UMAP caches are saved under coordinates/ and models/. The portable explorer includes 8,038 searchable peaks and measured six-stage curves, retaining absent combinations as missing and flagging pseudobulks below 20 cells. Quantitative dynamics still belong in the full accessibility space.</p></body></html>'''
 (OUT/'report.html').write_text(body)
if __name__=='__main__':main()
